# LINE ENDINGS, GLOBALLY: this tree is LF and stays LF on every platform.
#
# The rule below is a blanket one because the per-path list under it has now
# missed a path TWICE — first `recipes.md`, which is generated but does not carry
# the word "generated" in its name, and then `docs/reference/cli.md` and the
# embedded recipe builtins, which took down the first Windows CI run this
# repository ever had, on the day of the first release.
#
# The reason the list keeps missing things is that the property it is tracking is
# not "is this file generated". It is "does anything, anywhere, compare these
# bytes" — and in a tree whose tests compare committed output against output
# generated in memory, whose capabilities hash is taken over spec bytes, and whose
# fixtures are diffed whole, the honest answer is "assume yes". A Windows checkout
# with core.autocrlf=true (the default) rewrites LF -> CRLF, the in-memory side
# keeps LF, and the comparison fails on a tree that is otherwise identical.
#
# So: normalise to LF in the repository and check out LF everywhere. The specific
# `-text` rules below stay, and still win for the paths they name — they say the
# stronger thing, which is that git must not touch those bytes at all.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Generated and compared byte-for-byte against a copy built in memory.
docs/reference/cli.md -text

# Embedded in the binary, and the recipe index is generated FROM them.
pkg/aboard/recipes/builtin/** -text
recipes/** -text

# `-text` on the embedded tree is LOAD-BEARING, and the reason is worth stating
# exactly, because the obvious version of it names a command CI never runs.
#
# `capsHash` is computed over the BYTES of pkg/aboard/web/views/*.spec.json. A
# Windows checkout with `core.autocrlf=true` rewrites LF -> CRLF, so those bytes
# differ from the ones on Linux and the hash moves — for a tree that is otherwise
# identical.
#
# What actually FAILS is `make test` on the windows-latest runner in ci.yml, not
# `aboard capabilities --check` (no CI job runs that): the Go suite compares the
# COMMITTED views/controls.generated.js against a copy generated in memory from
# the specs, and the in-memory one has LF endings whatever the checkout did. Same
# shape for the recipe-index gate. `aboard capabilities --check` would fail too,
# on a developer's Windows machine, which is the case that has no CI to catch it.
#
# So: treat the whole embedded tree as text git must not touch.
pkg/aboard/web/** -text

# Same reason, plus these are fixtures compared byte-for-byte: the recipe
# precedence trees under testdata/, and the example board `aboard init
# --example` seeds from, which is embedded at pkg/aboard/example/ rather than
# living in testdata/ so that a `go install` binary carries it.
testdata/** -text
pkg/aboard/example/** -text

# The generated files are written by the binary and diffed against the working
# tree by `make caps`. A rewritten line ending would show as a diff on every
# checkout on the other platform. Both of them: the reference is
# `*.generated.md`, and the recipe index is `recipes.md`, which does not carry
# the word "generated" in its name and was therefore not covered here.
.claude/skills/aboard/references/*.generated.md -text
.claude/skills/aboard/references/recipes.md -text
